THE BOOL VERSION, CALLED TWO WAYS
  clean_bools(input, true, false)  "  hello world  "
  clean_bools(input, false, true)  "Hello World"
  Both compile. One of them is the call the author meant and the
  other is the arguments swapped, and nothing anywhere can tell
  you which -- the types are identical, so the compiler has no
  question to ask.

AT THE CALL SITE, WHICH IS WHERE IT IS READ
  clean_bools(s, true, false)      <- true what? false what?
  clean(s, Casing::Lower, Trimming::Keep)
  The second one is readable without opening the function. That
  is most of the value, and it costs nothing.

THE ENUM VERSION, WITH THE ARGUMENTS SWAPPED
  clean(input, Trimming::Trim, Casing::Lower)
  does not compile: expected `Casing`, found `Trimming`. The
  backwards call became a build error rather than a wrong answer
  in production.
  clean(input, Casing::Lower,    Trimming::Trim) = "hello world"
  clean(input, Casing::Preserve, Trimming::Keep) = "  Hello World  "

AND IT IS FREE
  size_of::<bool>()      1 byte
  size_of::<Casing>()    1 byte
  Two named variants cost exactly what two unnamed states cost.
  There is no runtime price for the check, because the check
  happened at compile time.

WHEN A bool IS STILL RIGHT
  When the name at the call site already says everything:
  `v.is_empty()`, `s.contains(c)`, `if found { ... }`. The rule
  is about PARAMETERS -- a bool you pass in is two unnamed states
  arriving somewhere the name is gone.
